const DATA={"summary": [{"title": "1. What software engineering is, and what has changed", "slides": "1–6", "lead": "The foundations of software engineering remain important even though development environments, user expectations and software delivery models have changed.", "blocks": [["Definition and foundation", "<strong>Software engineering<\/strong> is the systematic application of engineering approaches to the development, operation and maintenance of software (slide 5). “Systematic” means that teams apply disciplined methods rather than relying on ad-hoc coding. A software project still needs requirements, design, implementation, testing, operation and maintenance. What has changed is how these activities are organised and how rapidly they repeat. The lecture’s message is not that older engineering principles have disappeared; it is that the same principles must now be applied in a more complicated and continuously changing environment."], ["Yesterday → Today → Tomorrow", "The lecture summarises the progression as <strong>Yesterday: desktop → client/server → web<\/strong>; <strong>Today: cloud → mobile → AI → IoT → distributed systems<\/strong>; and <strong>Tomorrow: AI agents → AI-generated software → intelligent systems → unknown future developments<\/strong> (slide 4). For example, a desktop application might have operated on a single computer, whereas a present-day delivery platform may connect mobile clients, cloud services, payments, maps and live data. The purpose of this timeline is to show growth in connectedness, automation and engineering complexity, not to claim that all older technologies have stopped being used."], ["Traditional versus modern development", "In the <strong>traditional sequence<\/strong> shown in slide 6, requirements lead to design, implementation, testing, deployment and maintenance. This presents work as successive stages. In the <strong>modern model<\/strong>, requirements, development, testing, deployment, monitoring and feedback form a continuous loop. New observations after release can create further requirements, modifications and tests. Modern practice therefore treats deployment as part of an ongoing software lifecycle, rather than simply the final destination. These are illustrative models; a particular project can combine elements of both."], ["Exam-style comparison", "A strong answer explains both the <strong>continuity<\/strong> and the <strong>change<\/strong>: disciplined engineering and quality still matter, but technologies, operating contexts, stakeholder needs and release cycles have become more varied. For instance, an online banking system may require continuous security updates, monitoring and responsiveness to new user expectations. This is why the lecture calls software engineering an evolving discipline."]]}, {"title": "2. Why software engineering is evolving", "slides": "7–10", "lead": "Change is driven simultaneously by technology, business needs, user expectations and society, not by technology alone.", "blocks": [["The modern engineering environment", "Today’s applications may involve <strong>cloud computing, mobile platforms, APIs, microservices, DevOps, AI, IoT, edge computing, cybersecurity, big data and distributed teams<\/strong> (slide 7). An engineer may not be a specialist in every area, but must understand enough to collaborate with specialists and assess integration, risks and appropriateness. The range of technologies is one reason the course asks more than “How do we build software?” It also asks which approach might help and when it should be used (slide 8)."], ["Four forces driving change", "<strong>Technology:<\/strong> AI, cloud, IoT and edge introduce new capabilities. <strong>Business:<\/strong> faster delivery, digital transformation and platform-based business models require responsive systems. <strong>Users:<\/strong> people expect personalisation, always-available services and mobile-first access. <strong>Society:<\/strong> privacy, sustainability, accessibility and trust affect software adoption and acceptability. A good essay explains how these forces interact. For example, a business may want personalised recommendations, but users and society also expect appropriate privacy safeguards."], ["The speed-of-change chain", "Slide 10 gives the cause-and-effect chain <strong>Technology → New capability → New user expectation → New software problem<\/strong>. For example, when AI becomes accessible, users start expecting intelligent features; developers integrate AI; and new engineering concerns emerge, such as whether outputs are accurate and safe. A new capability therefore creates an opportunity to improve software and a new responsibility to understand its limitations."], ["Why this course matters", "The course aims to develop the ability to select, evaluate and apply emerging techniques rather than memorise product names. Knowing that a new technology exists is insufficient. Software engineers need to consider the problem it solves, where it fits, what it adds and how its risks will be managed."]]}, {"title": "3. The ten current software engineering trends", "slides": "11", "lead": "Slide 11 lists ten trends. Later slides expand on some; the others are named as important themes rather than explained in detail.", "blocks": [["Trends 1–5", "<strong>1. AI-assisted software development:<\/strong> AI supports tasks such as code and test generation. <strong>2. DevOps and continuous delivery:<\/strong> development and operations work together in an iterative lifecycle. <strong>3. Cloud-native development:<\/strong> software is designed for modern cloud-based operation. <strong>4. Microservices:<\/strong> application capabilities can be separated into smaller services with distinct responsibilities. <strong>5. Platform engineering:<\/strong> included as a trend on slide 11, referring in general to approaches that help teams use shared development and delivery capabilities; <em>the lecture does not provide a separate detailed definition of platform engineering<\/em>."], ["Trends 6–10", "<strong>6. Low-code/no-code:<\/strong> visual, component-based application development. <strong>7. Software supply-chain security:<\/strong> listed as a major trend; as a useful application example, it asks teams to consider the security of external software components and dependencies. <strong>8. Data-driven software engineering:<\/strong> listed in the lecture; teams can use relevant evidence and measurements to guide engineering decisions. <strong>9. Green/sustainable software engineering:<\/strong> listed as a trend connecting software choices with resource and environmental concerns. <strong>10. Human–AI collaboration:<\/strong> engineers increasingly supervise and evaluate AI-supported work. The explanations of trends 5, 7, 8 and 9 are illustrative elaborations; the slides mainly name them."], ["How to answer a list-based question", "If asked to <strong>identify<\/strong> trends, list the lecture’s terms accurately. If asked to <strong>discuss<\/strong> them, select relevant trends and explain both a use and an engineering implication. For example, AI assistance can accelerate test generation but requires validation, while cloud-native development can support scalability but introduces distributed-system complexity. Avoid claiming all ten trends are equally detailed in the lecture."]]}, {"title": "4. AI-assisted development and the changing engineer role", "slides": "12–13, 19, 27", "lead": "AI moves software engineering effort from generating every line manually towards specifying, reviewing, testing and coordinating outputs.", "blocks": [["Uses of AI-assisted software engineering", "Slide 12 lists <strong>code generation, code completion, test generation, bug detection, requirements analysis, documentation and code review<\/strong>. The GenAI slide also shows potential participation across requirements, design, coding, testing, documentation and maintenance (slide 19). For example, an engineer could ask an AI system to draft a set of test cases based on requirements, then check that those tests actually cover important situations. These are possible uses, not guarantees of correctness."], ["Old role versus emerging role", "The slide contrasts <strong>Old view: developer → writes code<\/strong> with <strong>Emerging view: developer → specifies, evaluates, verifies and orchestrates<\/strong> (slide 13). To specify is to describe clearly what a system needs; to evaluate is to judge a proposed output against requirements; to verify is to check its correctness through review and testing; and to orchestrate is to coordinate tools, tasks and work products. Engineers remain responsible for achieving dependable results even if a tool generates parts of the implementation."], ["Why AI does not eliminate software engineering", "Generated code can be <strong>incorrect, insecure or inappropriate<\/strong> (slide 27). Slide 24 additionally identifies hallucination and security as concerns with generative AI. Therefore, a developer must still understand the requirements, examine generated logic, test behaviour, consider security and decide whether the output is suitable for the deployment context. AI may reduce repetitive production effort, but it can increase the importance of checking assumptions and verifying results."], ["Worked application: AI-generated login code", "<em>Practice scenario:<\/em> an AI tool creates a login function in seconds. The engineer should inspect authentication behaviour, error handling and security rather than trusting it because it compiles. A functional test, review and appropriate checks are necessary before release. The lesson is to treat AI output as a candidate solution that requires engineering judgement."]]}, {"title": "5. DevOps, continuous delivery and lifecycle integration", "slides": "6, 14", "lead": "DevOps joins development and operations concerns and encourages a continuous flow from planning through feedback.", "blocks": [["Traditional versus DevOps team boundaries", "Slide 14 contrasts <strong>Traditional: Development | Operations<\/strong> with <strong>Modern: Development + Operations<\/strong>. In the older division, the people implementing a feature and the people operating it may work in separate organisational stages. DevOps encourages closer collaboration, so release, maintenance and operational feedback inform how software is developed. The goal is not merely faster coding, but an effective end-to-end delivery and learning process."], ["The full DevOps loop", "Memorise the slide’s sequence: <strong>Plan → Code → Build → Test → Release → Deploy → Monitor → Feedback<\/strong>. Planning decides what is needed; coding implements it; building prepares an executable; testing checks expected behaviour; releasing/deploying makes a version available; monitoring observes real operation; and feedback helps shape the next plan. Because the process repeats, issues found in production should become inputs into future engineering work."], ["Why DevOps matters", "The lecture emphasises that software engineering increasingly concerns <strong>the whole lifecycle, not coding alone<\/strong>. A release that appears successful at deployment may still fail under real conditions, so monitoring and feedback matter. An exam answer should connect DevOps to continuous requirements and maintenance (slide 6), and should avoid claiming that moving quickly removes the need for testing or security."]]}, {"title": "6. Cloud-native software, microservices and distributed complexity", "slides": "7, 11, 15, 24", "lead": "Cloud-native software brings together infrastructure and software patterns that support ongoing deployment, integration and changing demand.", "blocks": [["Key cloud-native elements", "Slide 15 identifies <strong>containers, microservices, APIs, elasticity, distributed systems and continuous deployment<\/strong>. A container packages software and required runtime elements for consistent execution. An API provides an interface through which components communicate. Elasticity means the deployed system can adjust resources in response to changing demand. Continuous deployment concerns making software updates part of an ongoing delivery process. These brief explanations unpack the slide terms and are intended as study explanations."], ["Monolithic versus separated services", "The example moves from a <strong>monolithic application<\/strong> to services labelled <strong>User, Payment, Recommendation, Notification and Order<\/strong> (slide 15). A monolith groups many functions within one application; microservices organise related capabilities into separate services that communicate. Separating services can make independent changes possible, but it does not make overall design automatically simpler: an order feature may now require several networked components to cooperate."], ["Benefits and concerns", "Cloud technology offers <strong>scalability<\/strong>, which slide 24 pairs with <strong>complexity<\/strong>. In a distributed design, engineers must consider communication between services, failures across boundaries, data consistency and monitoring across multiple components. These are practical examples of distributed complexity, not an exhaustive list given in the slides. The correct architectural decision depends on the project’s actual need, team skills and maintenance resources."], ["Practice comparison", "A small single-shop application may not need many independent network services. In contrast, a large application with separately changing user, payment and recommendation features may benefit from service boundaries. In both cases, the engineer should justify the design rather than assume microservices are automatically more modern and therefore better."]]}, {"title": "7. Low-code and no-code development", "slides": "11, 16, 24", "lead": "Visual development can accelerate software creation, but speed must be balanced with long-term engineering needs.", "blocks": [["What low-code/no-code means in the lecture", "Slide 16 highlights <strong>visual development, drag-and-drop components, citizen developers and rapid application development<\/strong>. Instead of manually writing every program element, users can create applications using ready-made tools and visual workflows. “Citizen developers” refers broadly to people creating solutions without necessarily being full-time professional software developers. This lowers the effort required for some routine applications but does not remove the need for sound requirements and testing."], ["Five engineering concerns", "The lecture lists <strong>security, scalability, maintainability, vendor lock-in and integration<\/strong>. Security concerns whether the application protects information and its operations. Scalability asks whether it can support increasing use. Maintainability asks whether future changes are manageable. Vendor lock-in occurs when dependence on one provider makes moving away difficult or costly. Integration concerns connecting the visual application to other systems. Slide 24 particularly highlights maintainability as a risk."], ["Evaluate before adopting", "A suitable exam recommendation is conditional. Low-code could be useful for a limited internal workflow with common requirements and a rapid deadline. However, the team should examine the platform’s constraints, future maintenance responsibilities, security requirements and integration needs. A tool that saves initial development time may create additional cost if the system becomes difficult to extend or migrate."]]}, {"title": "8. Emerging technologies: definition, groups and generative AI", "slides": "17–19", "lead": "An emerging technology is still developing in capability, application or adoption, while a current technology has become relatively mainstream.", "blocks": [["Current versus emerging", "Slide 17 defines <strong>current technology<\/strong> as already widely adopted and becoming mainstream, while <strong>emerging technology<\/strong> has capabilities, applications or adoption that are still developing. The distinction is contextual and can change over time. The slide gives <strong>generative AI, AI agents, digital twins, edge AI, quantum computing, extended reality and autonomous systems<\/strong> as examples. You should explain the meaning of “emerging” rather than simply saying “new”."], ["Four areas of the technology landscape", "The lecture groups technologies into <strong>AI<\/strong> (generative AI, AI agents, multimodal AI); <strong>Computing<\/strong> (edge computing, quantum computing, cloud); <strong>Connected systems<\/strong> (IoT, digital twins, autonomous systems); and <strong>Development<\/strong> (low-code, AI-assisted development, platform engineering). The same idea can connect to multiple areas. For example, a digital twin uses connected-system data while still requiring software engineering, testing and maintenance."], ["Generative AI across the lifecycle", "The lecture’s sequence is <strong>Requirements → Design → Coding → Testing → Documentation → Maintenance<\/strong>. Generative AI may assist at every stage, including generating requirements drafts, code, tests or documentation, and supporting review and analysis. The key question on slide 19 is <strong>“What could go wrong?”<\/strong> Accuracy, suitability and security must be checked. Generated material should be verified against the relevant engineering task, not accepted solely because it appears complete."]]}, {"title": "9. AI agents and digital twins", "slides": "20–21, 24", "lead": "Two emerging approaches change the relationship between software and real-world work: agents take steps toward goals, while digital twins track physical systems.", "blocks": [["Traditional AI versus agentic systems", "Slide 20 contrasts <strong>Traditional AI: User → AI → Answer<\/strong> with <strong>Agentic system: Goal → Plan → Use tools → Perform actions → Evaluate → Adapt<\/strong>. Rather than only answering a question, an agent may work through a multi-step task and adjust based on results. Within software engineering, this creates possibilities for automating portions of workflow. It also raises questions of permission, correctness, oversight and when people should approve consequential actions."], ["The benefit–risk pair for agents", "Slide 24 identifies <strong>automation<\/strong> as an agentic benefit and <strong>control/trust<\/strong> as concerns. A tool-using agent may complete more work than a simple suggestion engine, so an incorrect plan or an inappropriate tool action can have effects beyond a wrong text response. <em>Example for practice:<\/em> an agent helping with code maintenance could propose and run checks, but human review and suitable boundaries should be considered before changes are applied to a critical system."], ["Digital twin definition and examples", "A <strong>digital twin<\/strong> is a digital representation of a physical system that can be updated using data from the real system (slide 21). Examples given include <strong>factory, building, vehicle, city and healthcare equipment<\/strong>. A digital representation that stays connected to real-world information can help people observe or analyse an evolving system. The important characteristic in the lecture is the update relationship with a physical system, not merely a static drawing or a one-time model."], ["Digital twin engineering challenge", "The slide asks how software engineers can <strong>develop, integrate, test and maintain<\/strong> software connected to a continuously changing physical system. Updates may arrive from devices or other real-world data sources. The team must therefore consider how to keep the representation meaningful and ensure that the digital information remains reliable as the physical system changes. This is why a twin is an ongoing engineering responsibility rather than a one-off simulation."]]}, {"title": "10. Edge computing, quantum computing and new engineering questions", "slides": "22–25", "lead": "Technologies should be judged by what they solve and what additional engineering burdens they introduce.", "blocks": [["Cloud versus edge processing", "In the <strong>cloud<\/strong> path shown on slide 22, <strong>Device → Internet → Cloud<\/strong>. In the <strong>edge<\/strong> path, <strong>Device → Nearby computing → Cloud<\/strong>. Processing near the device can reduce the time required for a response and decrease the amount of data sent over the network. The lecture lists <strong>lower latency, reduced bandwidth, local processing and privacy considerations<\/strong> as reasons for edge use. An autonomous vehicle is the stated example of a situation requiring rapid local decisions."], ["Benefits and limitations of edge", "The main advantage is low latency, while slide 24 highlights <strong>distributed complexity<\/strong> as the new concern. Running processing in multiple nearby locations creates additional coordination, deployment and maintenance challenges. Edge computing can help with some privacy goals because data may be processed locally, but it does not automatically guarantee privacy or security. A strong answer explains the trade-off and connects it to the proposed use case."], ["Quantum computing", "Slide 23 lists <strong>optimisation, cryptography, simulation and machine learning<\/strong> as potential areas of interest. It explicitly warns that quantum computing is <strong>not simply “a faster computer”<\/strong>. New computing models may require different ways of designing algorithms and systems. An exam answer should not claim that quantum computers will make all programs faster; instead, focus on the potential suitability of specific problems and the need for new design thinking."], ["The hidden side of technology", "Slide 24 pairs each opportunity with a challenge: <strong>GenAI → faster development / hallucination and security; Cloud → scalability / complexity; IoT → connected systems / security; AI agents → automation / control and trust; Low-code → speed / maintainability; Edge → low latency / distributed complexity<\/strong>. Understanding both sides is essential. A tool’s benefit is not the end of its engineering evaluation."]]}, {"title": "11. The six technology-evaluation questions and exam method", "slides": "25–30", "lead": "Lecture 1 closes by teaching a repeatable way to assess any new tool or emerging technology.", "blocks": [["Six questions to memorise", "For <strong>any<\/strong> emerging technology, ask: <strong>(1)<\/strong> What problem does it solve? <strong>(2)<\/strong> How does it work? <strong>(3)<\/strong> When is it useful? <strong>(4)<\/strong> What are its limitations? <strong>(5)<\/strong> What new engineering problems does it create? <strong>(6)<\/strong> Can we realistically adopt it? (slide 25). These questions provide a ready-made structure for most of the lecture’s scenario and essay questions."], ["Technology Detective activity", "Slide 26 asks students to choose <strong>generative AI, AI agents, IoT, cloud, digital twins, low-code or edge computing<\/strong> and discuss the problem addressed, where it could be used, one benefit and one engineering challenge. These four prompts are a shorter version of the six-question evaluation framework. Practise answering them in complete sentences for more than one technology."], ["The lecture’s worked Generative AI case", "Slide 27 states that developers spend substantial time producing code and documentation. A possible application is to generate code, test cases and documentation. The benefit is a potential reduction in development effort, while the challenge is that output may be incorrect, insecure or inappropriate. The answer to <strong>“Would you trust AI-generated code without testing it?”<\/strong> is that generated code requires verification and testing before reliance."], ["The six skills in the course", "Slide 29 identifies <strong>Explore<\/strong> what is emerging; <strong>Understand<\/strong> how it works; <strong>Analyse<\/strong> the problem addressed; <strong>Evaluate<\/strong> opportunities and limitations; <strong>Apply<\/strong> where it can be used; and <strong>Synthesise<\/strong> how it could help solve real software-engineering problems. For essay work, this sequence encourages explanations that move from definition to reasoned recommendation."], ["How to write a structured answer", "Use <strong>Define → Explain → Example → Benefit → Challenge → Judgement<\/strong>. For an 8–12 mark scenario, begin by identifying the technology and problem. Explain how it works at a lecture-appropriate level, give a relevant scenario, describe at least one realistic benefit and risk, and conclude whether adoption is appropriate under stated conditions. The marks shown in this practice pack are suggested study prompts, not official lecturer marking allocations."]]}, {"title": "12. Ten essential takeaways to carry into the exam", "slides": "5–6, 10–30", "lead": "These ten statements bring together the lecture’s key arguments. They are explanatory revision takeaways, not a list printed together on any single slide.", "blocks": [["Takeaways 1–2: Stable principles and continuous development", "<strong>1. Software engineering principles remain constant while environments and tools evolve.<\/strong> The disciplined process of developing, operating and maintaining software remains necessary (slide 5). <strong>2. Modern development has shifted from a largely sequential, waterfall-like model to continuous, lifecycle-integrated approaches.<\/strong> The lecture contrasts staged delivery with a feedback loop covering requirements, development, testing, deployment and monitoring (slide 6)."], ["Takeaways 3–4: Opportunities and the AI-agent shift", "<strong>3. Emerging technologies create new opportunities alongside complex engineering challenges.<\/strong> The examples on slide 24 connect benefits to risks. <strong>4. AI is transitioning from code assistance towards agents that can take actions in development workflows.<\/strong> AI tools can generate code and tests, while agentic systems follow goals, plan, use tools, evaluate and adapt (slides 12 and 20)."], ["Takeaways 5–6: Engineer responsibilities and modern infrastructure", "<strong>5. The software engineer’s role is shifting from purely writing code toward specifying, orchestrating, verifying and evaluating systems.<\/strong> This is the central contrast on slide 13. <strong>6. Cloud-native development, microservices and DevOps are important elements of modern scalable software delivery.<\/strong> They connect infrastructure, service architecture and continuous lifecycle practice (slides 14–15). They are important trends, not mandatory choices for every project."], ["Takeaways 7–8: Low-code and edge", "<strong>7. Low-code/no-code can accelerate application creation but raises maintenance, scalability, security, integration and vendor-dependence concerns.<\/strong> Slide 16 describes both its visual development ideas and engineering concerns. <strong>8. Edge computing can reduce latency and network bandwidth by processing nearer to devices.<\/strong> It can support local decisions and some privacy considerations, while introducing distributed complexity (slides 22 and 24)."], ["Takeaways 9–10: Digital twins and assessment", "<strong>9. Digital twins allow a digital representation to be updated with data from a changing physical system.<\/strong> Engineers must develop, integrate, test and maintain that connection (slide 21). <strong>10. Every new technology requires assessment of its limitations, risks, integration and realistic adoption.<\/strong> Slide 25’s six-question checklist provides the method. The overall lecture message is that engineers must understand <em>what<\/em> technology does and <em>when and how<\/em> to apply it (slide 30)."], ["The exact three take-home messages", "Slide 30 closes with: <strong>(1) Software engineering is evolving.<\/strong> <strong>(2) Emerging technologies create new possibilities—and new challenges.<\/strong> <strong>(3) Software engineers must understand not only WHAT a technology does, but WHEN and HOW it should be applied.<\/strong> Use this three-sentence idea to finish a broad essay on Lecture 1."]]}], "essays": [{"topic": "Foundations", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 8, "slides": "4–5", "question": "Define software engineering and explain why its fundamental principles remain relevant despite technological change.", "points": ["Correct lecture definition", "Disciplined development, operation and maintenance", "Stable engineering principles", "Changing environment and suitable example"], "answer": "Software engineering is the systematic application of engineering approaches to the development, operation and maintenance of software. It means using disciplined processes and technical judgement to create software that can work reliably and be maintained over time.\n\nThe fundamental engineering principles remain relevant because every system still needs clearly understood requirements, appropriate design, implementation, testing and maintenance. However, the environment in which those principles are applied has changed. Modern applications may use cloud services, mobile devices, AI, IoT and distributed systems rather than a single desktop program. This increases integration and operational challenges.\n\nFor example, an online service still needs software testing, but it may also need continuous monitoring after deployment because users interact with it throughout the day. Therefore, software engineering has not lost its foundations. Instead, engineers must apply those foundations to newer technologies and continuously evolving systems."}, {"topic": "Foundations", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 10, "slides": "4–6", "question": "Compare traditional and modern software engineering processes. Explain why continuous feedback has become important.", "points": ["Traditional sequence", "Modern feedback loop", "Clear operational distinction", "Benefits of repeated feedback", "Concrete example"], "answer": "The traditional software engineering process shown in the lecture follows a mainly sequential pattern: requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment and maintenance. This representation suggests that work progresses through successive stages before the product enters long-term use.\n\nThe modern process places continuous requirements, development, testing, deployment, monitoring and feedback within a repeating cycle. After software has been deployed, monitoring may reveal errors or changing user needs. This evidence can generate new requirements and lead to another round of development and testing.\n\nFor example, a mobile banking application might receive feedback that its payment confirmation screen is confusing. The development team can use that feedback to refine requirements, update the interface, test it and deploy an improvement. Continuous feedback helps a system respond to real usage rather than relying only on assumptions made before release. The two models illustrate different approaches; modern practice does not remove the importance of disciplined requirements, testing or maintenance."}, {"topic": "Foundations", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 8, "slides": "4, 7", "question": "Describe the evolution from yesterday’s software to today’s software and the emerging direction suggested by the lecture.", "points": ["Yesterday: desktop/client-server/web", "Today: cloud/mobile/AI/IoT/distributed", "Tomorrow: agents/AI generated/intelligent", "Engineering implications"], "answer": "The lecture describes yesterday’s software as progressing from desktop applications to client/server systems and then web-based applications. This development expanded software access from a local computer toward connected users and systems.\n\nToday’s environment includes cloud computing, mobile applications, artificial intelligence, the Internet of Things and distributed systems. Many current services combine several of these technologies. For example, a ride-hailing application may use a mobile interface, online maps, cloud processing, digital payment services and data shared between multiple components.\n\nThe lecture identifies AI agents, AI-generated software and intelligent systems as possible future directions. These approaches may automate more complex tasks, but they also create new questions about correctness, control and trust. Overall, software engineering is moving toward increasingly connected and adaptive systems. Engineers still need the core skills of requirements analysis, design, testing and maintenance to manage this complexity."}, {"topic": "Drivers", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "8–10", "question": "Analyse four factors that are driving change in modern software engineering and provide an example of how they interact.", "points": ["Technology", "Business", "Users", "Society", "Integrated scenario"], "answer": "Modern software engineering is shaped by four major forces: technology, business needs, user expectations and society. Technology introduces capabilities such as AI, cloud computing, IoT and edge processing. These capabilities allow developers to build functions that were previously difficult or expensive.\n\nBusinesses want faster delivery, digital transformation and participation in platform-based markets, so they often adopt tools and processes that shorten the time needed to deliver useful software. Users increasingly expect personalised, mobile-first and continuously available services. At the same time, society expects attention to privacy, accessibility, sustainability and trust.\n\nThese forces interact. For example, a company may introduce AI recommendations because the technology is available and customers want more personalised experiences. The business expects higher engagement, but engineers must also consider how information is collected and whether recommendations can be trusted. Therefore, engineering decisions cannot be based on technological capability alone; they must balance practical business needs, users and wider concerns."}, {"topic": "Drivers", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 8, "slides": "10", "question": "Explain the chain “technology → new capability → new user expectation → new software problem” using an AI-based application.", "points": ["Correct chain", "AI capability", "New expectation", "Engineering difficulty", "Conclusion"], "answer": "The lecture explains that a new technology often creates a new capability, which changes what users expect from software. Meeting that expectation then introduces a new engineering problem.\n\nFor example, widely available generative AI makes it possible to build applications that answer questions and produce written content. Once users experience this capability, they may expect an educational platform to provide instant explanations and tailored learning support. Developers then face the challenge of integrating an AI feature into the platform.\n\nHowever, the new function introduces concerns about incorrect or misleading outputs, security and whether generated information is suitable for the learner. Engineers must define requirements, evaluate the generated answers and test how the feature behaves. Thus, technological progress creates benefits and additional engineering responsibilities simultaneously. The correct question is not only whether AI is available, but whether it can be integrated dependably to solve the intended problem."}, {"topic": "Trends", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "11", "question": "Identify the ten current software engineering trends named in the lecture and explain any four of them.", "points": ["List all ten lecture terms", "Explain AI assistance", "Explain DevOps", "Explain cloud-native/microservices", "Explain one other trend"], "answer": "The ten current trends identified in the lecture are AI-assisted software development; DevOps and continuous delivery; cloud-native development; microservices; platform engineering; low-code/no-code; software supply-chain security; data-driven software engineering; green or sustainable software engineering; and human–AI collaboration.\n\nAI-assisted development allows software engineers to use AI in coding, test generation, documentation and related tasks, but the results must still be checked. DevOps and continuous delivery bring development and operational activities together in a lifecycle of planning, building, testing, deploying, monitoring and feedback. Cloud-native development combines capabilities such as containers, APIs, distributed systems and elasticity to support modern deployments. Microservices organise a system into smaller service components, such as separate payment and order functions.\n\nLow-code/no-code is another important trend, allowing rapid visual development through ready-made components. However, security and maintainability remain engineering concerns. All these trends show that current software engineering involves more than writing program code; it also includes selecting approaches and managing their consequences."}, {"topic": "AI-assisted", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "12–13", "question": "Explain seven ways AI can assist software development and discuss how AI changes the developer’s role.", "points": ["Seven uses from slide 12", "Old role", "New specification/evaluation/verification/orchestration role", "Need for human responsibility"], "answer": "AI-assisted software engineering can support code generation, code completion, test generation, bug detection, requirements analysis, documentation and code review. For example, AI may suggest code for a common feature or draft tests that the engineer can inspect and improve.\n\nThe lecture contrasts an older view of a developer primarily writing code with an emerging view in which the developer specifies, evaluates, verifies and orchestrates work. Specifying means communicating precise requirements and constraints. Evaluating means deciding whether an output meets the intended need. Verifying means checking accuracy, security and behaviour using suitable engineering methods. Orchestration means coordinating tasks and tools so they contribute to a coherent result.\n\nThis change does not mean software engineers are unnecessary. AI output may be incorrect, insecure or unsuitable. Engineers still need domain understanding, technical competence and accountability to ensure that the software works as required. AI changes where effort is spent, not the need for engineering judgement."}, {"topic": "AI-assisted", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "12–13, 24, 27", "question": "A team generates most of its application code with AI. Evaluate whether the team can deploy the output without testing.", "points": ["No automatic trust", "Incorrect code and hallucination", "Security and suitability", "Review/testing steps", "Conditional judgement"], "answer": "The team should not deploy AI-generated code solely because it was produced quickly or appears functional. The lecture states that generative AI can reduce development effort, but its output may be incorrect, insecure or inappropriate. It also identifies hallucination and security as new concerns.\n\nAI tools can produce convincing explanations or code while misunderstanding requirements or overlooking unusual situations. In a real application, such mistakes may cause failures or security problems. Engineers must verify that the code solves the intended problem and matches the system’s design.\n\nA responsible approach is to review the generated implementation, test its expected behaviour, check how it handles errors and examine relevant security risks before release. The team should also confirm that changes integrate correctly with existing components. AI can be used to speed up parts of development, but it cannot replace human evaluation and verification. Therefore, deployment should depend on sufficient engineering evidence, not on how rapidly the code was generated."}, {"topic": "AI-assisted", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 8, "slides": "12–13, 19–20", "question": "Differentiate AI-assisted development, generative AI and AI agents in the context of software engineering.", "points": ["AI assistance as engineering use", "GenAI output generation", "Agents goal/tool/action cycle", "Different risks"], "answer": "AI-assisted development refers to using AI to support software engineering activities such as completing code, generating tests, reviewing code or preparing documentation. It describes the use of AI within development work rather than a single specific technology.\n\nGenerative AI is a capability that creates outputs such as code, text or test cases from an input. In the lecture, generative AI may participate across the lifecycle from requirements to maintenance. Its output still needs checking because it may be incorrect or insecure.\n\nAn AI agent goes further by working toward a goal through planning, tool use, actions, evaluation and adaptation. Instead of producing only an answer, an agent may participate in a multi-step workflow. This makes control, authorisation and trust especially important.\n\nAll three concepts are related but not identical. The engineering challenge becomes greater when AI moves from suggesting an output to carrying out actions that change a software project."}, {"topic": "AI-assisted", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "19, 27", "question": "Discuss how generative AI can contribute at different stages of the software development lifecycle and what may go wrong.", "points": ["Full lifecycle coverage", "Concrete tasks", "Efficiency benefit", "Incorrect/insecure/inappropriate outputs", "Safeguards"], "answer": "The lecture shows that generative AI can be involved in requirements, design, coding, testing, documentation and maintenance. During requirements work it may help draft or analyse specifications. During implementation it can generate code, while during testing it may produce draft test cases. It may also help prepare documentation and support review and analysis.\n\nThe main opportunity is a potential reduction in time spent producing routine code and written material. For example, engineers may create an initial version of documentation quickly and then refine it to match the real system.\n\nHowever, generated outputs may contain mistakes, unsafe code or explanations that do not fit project requirements. A generated test may also miss an important case. Therefore, engineers must assess the accuracy and suitability of every relevant output through review, testing and comparison with actual requirements. Generative AI can support the full lifecycle, but it does not guarantee the quality of that lifecycle."}, {"topic": "DevOps", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "6, 14", "question": "Define DevOps and explain the eight stages of the continuous DevOps flow in the lecture.", "points": ["Development + operations", "Plan/code/build/test", "Release/deploy", "Monitor/feedback", "Continuous loop value"], "answer": "DevOps is an approach that brings development and operations into closer collaboration across the software lifecycle. The lecture contrasts the traditional separation between development and operations with a modern integrated approach.\n\nIts continuous flow begins with Plan, in which the team decides what must be delivered. Code involves implementation, followed by Build, where software is prepared for use. Test checks whether the software behaves as required. Release prepares the chosen version for delivery, while Deploy makes it available in the intended environment. Monitor observes software behaviour during operation, and Feedback provides information for the next planning and development cycle.\n\nThese stages matter because software does not become an engineering success simply when it is installed. Users, operational conditions and requirements may change. DevOps encourages teams to learn from deployment and improve software continuously while maintaining appropriate quality checks. In this way, software engineering becomes a lifecycle activity rather than only a coding task."}, {"topic": "DevOps", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 8, "slides": "6, 14", "question": "Compare the traditional development–operations division with the DevOps approach.", "points": ["Traditional separation", "DevOps collaboration", "Continuous flow", "Monitoring/feedback", "Practical example"], "answer": "In the traditional organisation shown in the lecture, development and operations are separated. Developers focus on creating software, while operations personnel take greater responsibility once the software is deployed. This separation may make communication across stages more difficult.\n\nDevOps instead presents development and operations as closely connected. The team considers planning, coding, building, testing, releasing, deploying, monitoring and feedback as parts of one continuous process. Operational information can therefore influence future development decisions.\n\nFor example, after a shopping application is deployed, monitoring may reveal that the checkout function becomes slow during busy periods. Under a collaborative approach, development and operations can use those observations to identify the problem, implement an improvement and test it before the next release.\n\nThe main difference is not that DevOps removes testing or maintenance. It brings those activities into an ongoing cycle so the system can improve in response to real conditions."}, {"topic": "Cloud", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "15", "question": "Explain six characteristics of cloud-native software presented in the lecture.", "points": ["Containers", "Microservices", "APIs", "Elasticity", "Distributed systems", "Continuous deployment"], "answer": "Cloud-native development commonly involves containers, microservices, APIs, elasticity, distributed systems and continuous deployment. Containers help package an application and its runtime needs into a consistent unit. Microservices organise features into smaller, separately defined services, while APIs allow those services or other applications to communicate.\n\nElasticity means adjusting computing resources as demand changes. Distributed systems allow software components to operate across different connected computing environments. Continuous deployment supports ongoing delivery of software updates rather than relying only on infrequent major releases.\n\nFor example, an online commerce platform might separate order processing, payment and notifications into services connected by APIs. The system may need additional resources when many customers shop at once. However, this design also introduces complexity because components must communicate reliably.\n\nTherefore, cloud-native technologies can help a project scale and evolve, but the architecture should be justified by the application’s real needs and engineering capabilities."}, {"topic": "Cloud", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 10, "slides": "15, 24", "question": "Compare monolithic software and microservices using the lecture’s user, payment, recommendation, notification and order example.", "points": ["Monolithic grouping", "Service separation", "Example component names", "Potential advantage", "Complexity risk"], "answer": "A monolithic application contains multiple functions within one application structure. The lecture illustrates a move from a monolithic application toward smaller services labelled User, Payment, Recommendation, Notification and Order.\n\nWith microservices, those capabilities are organised as separate services. For example, the Order service may coordinate order information while the Payment service handles payment-related functionality. Communication between services can occur through APIs. This separation may allow particular services to change or scale independently when the system and team are designed for it.\n\nHowever, the architecture also becomes a distributed system in which several components must communicate. A failure in one service can affect a larger workflow, and integration, testing and maintenance may be more complex. The lecture explicitly pairs cloud scalability with complexity.\n\nTherefore, microservices are not automatically the best architecture for every project. An engineer should consider whether the benefits of independent services justify the added coordination and operational responsibilities."}, {"topic": "Cloud", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "15, 24–25", "question": "A small campus café wants to split its simple POS system into ten cloud microservices. Evaluate this decision.", "points": ["Problem and scale", "Cloud-native possible benefits", "Integration and operations complexity", "Skills/resources", "Balanced recommendation"], "answer": "The decision should be evaluated according to the café’s actual problem rather than assuming that ten microservices are automatically better because they are a modern trend. Cloud-native systems can provide elasticity, distributed deployment and the ability to separate responsibilities into services. These benefits are useful when the application has demanding scale or independently changing components.\n\nHowever, a simple point-of-sale system may have relatively limited requirements. Splitting it into many networked services can create additional communication, deployment, testing and maintenance work. The team must understand distributed-system failures and how the services will be monitored. The lecture specifically warns that cloud scalability comes with complexity.\n\nI would first identify the current performance, integration and maintenance requirements. A simpler architecture may be more realistic if it meets those needs with fewer operational risks. If clear growth or integration requirements arise, selected service boundaries could be considered. The best choice is the one that solves the actual problem with manageable engineering effort."}, {"topic": "Low-code", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "16, 24", "question": "Discuss low-code/no-code development, its advantages and five engineering concerns.", "points": ["Visual drag/drop and citizen developers", "Rapid development", "Security", "Scalability and maintainability", "Vendor lock-in and integration"], "answer": "Low-code and no-code platforms support application creation using visual development tools and drag-and-drop components. The lecture also refers to citizen developers, meaning people who create applications even though they may not be professional programmers. One advantage is rapid application development because common functions can be assembled without manually writing every line of code.\n\nHowever, the lecture identifies five important engineering concerns. Security remains necessary because an application may process important information. Scalability asks whether the platform can support larger workloads. Maintainability concerns the ability to modify and support the application over time. Vendor lock-in occurs when dependence on a particular platform makes migration difficult. Integration asks whether the application can work properly with existing systems.\n\nFor example, a visual form-based application could be useful for a small internal request process, but the team should investigate whether it can integrate with the organisation’s database and remain supportable later. Thus, rapid development must be balanced with long-term engineering quality."}, {"topic": "Low-code", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "16, 25", "question": "A department wants to build an internal staff-request app entirely with no-code tools. Should it proceed?", "points": ["Identify appropriate problem", "Rapid visual development", "Security/integration/maintenance", "Vendor dependence and growth", "Conditional decision"], "answer": "No-code tools may be suitable if the department needs a straightforward staff-request application with standard forms, approval steps and simple reporting. The lecture highlights rapid development, visual components and citizen developers as strengths of low-code/no-code approaches. These features could reduce initial implementation effort.\n\nHowever, the department should assess the data involved, the number of users and the need to connect with existing systems. Security matters if staff details are collected. Integration matters if requests must flow to other applications. Maintainability is important because approval rules and business requirements may change. The department should also understand vendor lock-in and whether it can move the application to another platform if needed.\n\nI would recommend a limited trial only after checking these requirements and constraints. If the platform supports the required security, integration and future changes, it may be a practical solution. If those risks cannot be managed, another development approach may be more appropriate."}, {"topic": "Emerging", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 8, "slides": "17–18", "question": "Distinguish between current and emerging technologies, and give examples from the lecture.", "points": ["Current is mainstream", "Emerging developing capabilities/applications/adoption", "Several lecture examples", "Difference changes over time"], "answer": "A current technology is one that has already become widely adopted and is moving into mainstream use. An emerging technology is one whose capabilities, applications or level of adoption are still developing. This does not mean that an emerging technology must have been invented recently; its practical role may still be changing.\n\nThe lecture identifies generative AI, AI agents, digital twins, edge AI, quantum computing, extended reality and autonomous systems as examples of emerging technologies. It also groups technologies across AI, computing, connected systems and software development.\n\nFor example, cloud computing is already used across many software environments, while some forms of agentic AI are still developing in how they plan, use tools and perform actions. The distinction is important because engineers should not assume that a promising technology is already mature enough for every use case.\n\nTherefore, engineers should evaluate capabilities, limitations and realistic adoption rather than selecting tools solely because they are described as new or emerging."}, {"topic": "Emerging", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "18", "question": "Describe the four groups in the emerging technology landscape and give relevant examples.", "points": ["AI examples", "Computing examples", "Connected systems", "Development approaches", "Integration implication"], "answer": "The lecture divides the emerging technology landscape into four areas: AI, Computing, Connected Systems and Development. The AI category includes generative AI, AI agents and multimodal AI. Computing includes edge computing, quantum computing and cloud. Connected Systems includes IoT, digital twins and autonomous systems. Development includes low-code, AI-assisted development and platform engineering.\n\nThese groupings show that technologies can change different parts of a software system. AI may influence decision-making or content generation. Computing technologies affect where and how processing occurs. Connected systems involve devices and changing physical environments. Development technologies change the way engineers create, test and maintain software.\n\nFor example, a digital twin might use data from IoT devices and process some information at the edge, while its supporting software is developed using modern engineering practices. The categories therefore help organise the landscape, but real applications often combine several technologies. Engineers must evaluate integration as well as the individual benefits of each component."}, {"topic": "Agents", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 10, "slides": "20", "question": "Compare a traditional AI interaction with an agentic system, focusing on workflow and engineering concerns.", "points": ["User → AI → Answer", "Goal → Plan → Tools → Actions → Evaluate → Adapt", "Greater autonomy", "Control/trust risk", "Appropriate oversight"], "answer": "The lecture represents a traditional AI interaction as User → AI → Answer. In this arrangement, a person asks a question and the system returns a response. The main task is generating an answer to the prompt.\n\nAn agentic system is represented as Goal → Plan → Use tools → Perform actions → Evaluate → Adapt. Instead of only providing a response, the system works through several stages to pursue an objective. For example, a development agent might receive a goal, plan tasks, use tools to inspect files, perform selected actions, evaluate results and modify its plan when necessary.\n\nThis creates new opportunities for automation, but it also raises control and trust concerns. An incorrect answer can mislead the user, while an incorrect action may change files or affect a workflow. Therefore, engineers need to consider appropriate permissions, verification and human oversight. AI agents may assist software teams, but their ability to act increases the importance of responsible engineering boundaries."}, {"topic": "Agents", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "13, 20, 24–25", "question": "Your team wants an AI agent to modify production code without human review. Evaluate the risks and propose an approach.", "points": ["Benefit: automation", "Agent goal/action sequence", "Control/trust and wrong actions", "Verification/permissions", "Conditional deployment"], "answer": "An AI agent could make parts of software maintenance more efficient because it can plan, use tools, perform actions and evaluate results toward a goal. However, allowing it to modify production code without review introduces significant control and trust concerns, which the lecture explicitly associates with AI agents.\n\nAn agent might misunderstand a requirement, choose an unsuitable action or produce a change that appears successful but harms another component. Since the system performs actions rather than merely suggesting code, the possible effects may be more direct. The team still has responsibility for evaluating, verifying and orchestrating the engineering process.\n\nI would begin with clearly bounded tasks and require changes to be checked against requirements and relevant tests. Actions affecting important production functions should have suitable approval or review rather than being accepted automatically. The decision to expand automation should depend on evidence that the agent behaves reliably within defined limits.\n\nTherefore, agentic development can be useful, but the degree of autonomy must reflect the risks of the work."}, {"topic": "Twins", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "21", "question": "Define a digital twin and explain how it differs from a static digital representation of a physical object.", "points": ["Digital representation", "Updated using real-system data", "Examples", "Continuous changes", "Integration/testing/maintenance"], "answer": "A digital twin is a digital representation of a physical system that can be updated using data from the real system. The essential point in the lecture is that the representation is connected to information about a physical object or environment and can change as that system changes.\n\nA static digital drawing may show what a factory, building or vehicle looks like at one moment, but it does not necessarily reflect subsequent changes. A digital twin may be updated when new information arrives from the physical system. The lecture names factories, buildings, vehicles, cities and healthcare equipment as examples.\n\nFor example, a digital twin of a building could be updated using operating information from the real building. Engineers would then need to integrate data sources, test that the representation behaves as intended and maintain the software as the building’s state changes. Therefore, a digital twin is not just a visual model; it is an ongoing software-engineering problem involving a changing physical system."}, {"topic": "Twins", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "21, 25", "question": "A smart factory plans to use a digital twin. Analyse its possible value and engineering challenges.", "points": ["Appropriate factory application", "Real-world data updates", "Potential operational value", "Integration/testing/maintenance", "Recommendation"], "answer": "A digital twin could be useful for a smart factory because it provides a digital representation of a physical system that can be updated with information from the real factory. This could help people observe system conditions and reason about how the factory is operating.\n\nThe potential value is that software could reflect changes in the physical environment rather than relying only on a one-time diagram. For example, updated information about a machine could help staff understand its current state. However, the lecture stresses that developing, integrating, testing and maintaining a digital twin is difficult because the physical system continuously changes.\n\nEngineers would need to identify appropriate data sources and ensure the digital representation is updated meaningfully. They would also need to test the integrated software and maintain it as equipment or operating requirements change. I would recommend defining the actual factory problem first, then checking whether the benefits justify the integration and support effort. A digital twin should be adopted because it solves a real problem, not only because it is an emerging technology."}, {"topic": "Edge", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 10, "slides": "22", "question": "Compare cloud and edge processing using the data paths shown in the lecture.", "points": ["Cloud path", "Edge path", "Lower latency", "Bandwidth/local processing/privacy", "Distributed complexity"], "answer": "In the cloud model shown in the lecture, processing follows Device → Internet → Cloud. The device sends information across a network to a remote cloud environment where relevant processing may occur. In the edge model, the path is Device → Nearby computing → Cloud. Some processing happens closer to where the data is generated.\n\nEdge processing can lower latency because it reduces reliance on a long round trip to a remote service for certain tasks. It can also reduce bandwidth requirements when not all raw information needs to be transmitted. The lecture additionally identifies local processing and privacy considerations as reasons to consider edge computing.\n\nFor example, a vehicle requiring rapid decisions may benefit from nearby processing rather than waiting for all decisions to come from a distant cloud. However, edge systems introduce distributed complexity because processing is spread across multiple locations.\n\nThus, cloud and edge approaches should be selected according to timing, connectivity, privacy and operational needs rather than treating one as universally superior."}, {"topic": "Edge", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "22, 24–25", "question": "An autonomous vehicle needs rapid local decisions. Explain why edge computing may be useful and what risks remain.", "points": ["Low latency problem", "Local processing", "Reduced bandwidth", "Distributed complexity", "Balanced conclusion"], "answer": "An autonomous vehicle may need to react quickly to changing conditions, so latency is an important engineering consideration. The lecture specifically uses autonomous vehicles as an example of why rapid local decisions may be required.\n\nEdge computing processes information at or near the device rather than depending entirely on a remote cloud response. This can reduce delay for selected operations and reduce the amount of information that must travel across the network. Some data may also be handled locally, which can be relevant when considering privacy.\n\nHowever, edge computing does not eliminate engineering risk. The lecture identifies distributed complexity as its main new concern. Engineers must still develop, test and maintain components that operate across local and cloud environments, and ensure the overall design meets its intended needs.\n\nTherefore, edge processing may be appropriate for fast local decisions, but adoption should be based on the required response time and the ability to manage the additional distributed-system challenges."}, {"topic": "Quantum", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 8, "slides": "23", "question": "Why is quantum computing not simply “a faster computer”? Discuss its potential relevance to software engineers.", "points": ["Correct important distinction", "New computing model", "Potential application areas", "Different algorithm/system designs"], "answer": "The lecture explicitly states that quantum computing is not simply a faster version of a conventional computer. It represents a different computing model, so its potential value may depend on how particular problems and algorithms are designed.\n\nSlide 23 identifies optimisation, cryptography, simulation and machine learning as possible areas of interest. These are potential application areas rather than guarantees that quantum computing will outperform current systems in every task. For software engineers, the important implication is that new computing models may require new approaches to algorithm and system design.\n\nFor example, an engineer assessing a difficult optimisation problem should first understand whether a quantum-based method is relevant instead of assuming ordinary application code will automatically execute more quickly. The lecture’s broader principle applies here: engineers must investigate what the technology can do, its limitations and when it is useful. Quantum computing should therefore be evaluated for suitability, not treated as a universal performance upgrade."}, {"topic": "Risks", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 12, "slides": "24", "question": "Choose four technologies from the lecture and analyse one benefit and one engineering concern for each.", "points": ["Four correct technology–benefit pairs", "Four correct technology–risk pairs", "Explain consequences", "Appropriate engineering response"], "answer": "Generative AI can accelerate development by producing code, tests and documentation. However, generated outputs may hallucinate or introduce security problems, so engineers must review and verify them.\n\nCloud computing can support scalability as software demand grows. Its concern is increased complexity, particularly when systems are divided into networked components that must communicate and be operated reliably.\n\nIoT allows software to interact with connected devices. The lecture identifies security as a concern because connected systems create additional points where protection must be considered.\n\nAI agents offer automation by planning, using tools and performing actions toward a goal. Their new concern is control and trust, because the engineer must decide which actions the agent should be allowed to take and how results should be assessed.\n\nThe lecture also presents low-code as faster development with maintainability concerns, and edge computing as low-latency processing with distributed complexity. Across all cases, the same pattern applies: a useful capability creates new responsibilities that must be considered before adoption."}, {"topic": "Risks", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 12, "slides": "24–25", "question": "“Every new technology brings benefits and new engineering challenges.” Evaluate the statement with examples.", "points": ["Explain benefit/challenge principle", "At least three accurate examples", "Why benefits not enough", "Six-question framework", "Justified conclusion"], "answer": "I agree that emerging technologies create new opportunities and new engineering challenges at the same time. A technology can make a task faster or enable a new feature, but it also changes what engineers must design, integrate, test and maintain.\n\nFor example, generative AI may reduce coding effort, but its outputs can be incorrect or insecure. Cloud systems offer scalability but create distributed complexity. AI agents can automate tasks, but their ability to perform actions raises questions about control and trust. Edge computing can reduce latency for local decisions, but introduces complexity across distributed processing locations.\n\nThese examples show why selecting the newest tool is not sufficient. Engineers should identify the problem it solves, understand how it works, decide when it is useful, recognise its limitations, examine the new engineering difficulties and assess whether realistic adoption is possible.\n\nTherefore, responsible adoption requires balancing the expected benefit against the cost and risk of integration. The strongest engineering decision is one supported by a real need and an explanation of how new problems will be handled."}, {"topic": "Evaluation", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 12, "slides": "25", "question": "Explain the six questions software engineers should ask before adopting an emerging technology.", "points": ["Problem", "Mechanism", "When useful", "Limitations", "New engineering problems", "Realistic adoption"], "answer": "The lecture proposes six questions for evaluating any emerging technology. First, what problem does it solve? This ensures that adoption begins with an actual engineering or user need rather than technological excitement. Second, how does it work? Engineers need a basic understanding of the method to assess whether it can fit their system.\n\nThird, when is it useful? A technology may help in some conditions but add unnecessary complexity in others. Fourth, what are its limitations? This helps the team recognise cases in which the solution may fail or perform poorly. Fifth, what new engineering problems does it create? Examples include security concerns, integration difficulties, maintainability and distributed complexity.\n\nSixth, can we realistically adopt it? This requires considering available skills, resources and the work involved in implementation and maintenance. Together, these questions help engineers make justified choices. They shift the discussion from “Is this technology new?” to “Does it solve the problem effectively, and can we manage its consequences?”"}, {"topic": "Evaluation", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 12, "slides": "25–27", "question": "Apply the six technology-evaluation questions to using generative AI for code and test generation.", "points": ["Problem and working concept", "Useful cases", "Limitations", "Engineering risks", "Realistic adoption conditions"], "answer": "Generative AI may solve the problem of developers spending substantial time producing routine code, test cases and documentation. It works by generating proposed outputs from prompts or other inputs, allowing developers to prepare drafts more quickly.\n\nIt may be useful when a team needs assistance with repetitive coding tasks, early documentation drafts or initial test ideas. Its limitations include the possibility that generated code will not match the requirements or that tests will miss important cases. The lecture identifies incorrect, insecure and inappropriate output as challenges, and also names hallucination and security as concerns.\n\nNew engineering work is therefore needed to review, test and integrate generated material safely. To adopt generative AI realistically, a team must have engineers who understand the application well enough to check the output and have time for verification. I would recommend using it as a support tool while retaining professional responsibility for the final code. This approach gains possible efficiency without assuming that generation itself establishes software quality."}, {"topic": "Evaluation", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 12, "slides": "25–26", "question": "Use the Technology Detective activity to evaluate edge computing for a smart traffic application.", "points": ["Identify problem", "Explain use", "Benefit", "Engineering challenge", "Reasoned recommendation"], "answer": "A smart traffic application may need to process information quickly to respond to conditions near a road or junction. Edge computing could help because it allows some processing to take place near the devices that produce the data instead of sending every decision to a distant cloud environment.\n\nA suitable application could be local traffic monitoring that reacts to changing conditions. The expected benefit is lower latency, which may be important when the system must respond quickly. Reduced network bandwidth is another possible advantage because some information can be processed nearby.\n\nHowever, the lecture identifies distributed complexity as an engineering concern. The team may need to operate and maintain computing components across several locations and ensure that they work together appropriately. The engineers must also assess whether local processing is required for the actual application.\n\nI would recommend exploring edge computing if faster local responses are an important requirement and the team can manage its distributed design. Otherwise, a simpler approach may be more practical."}, {"topic": "Evaluation", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 10, "slides": "14–16, 25", "question": "A team must choose between cloud-native development, DevOps and low-code. Explain why these are not interchangeable solutions.", "points": ["Different conceptual purposes", "Cloud-native architecture", "DevOps lifecycle", "Low-code creation approach", "Choose against problem"], "answer": "Cloud-native development, DevOps and low-code/no-code are related software engineering trends, but they address different aspects of building and operating software. Cloud-native development concerns the design and operation of software using patterns such as containers, microservices, APIs, elasticity and distributed systems.\n\nDevOps concerns collaboration and the ongoing lifecycle of planning, coding, building, testing, releasing, deploying, monitoring and feedback. It is an approach to delivering and improving software, not a particular application architecture. Low-code/no-code concerns how applications can be created using visual components and rapid development tools.\n\nFor example, an organisation might use a low-code platform to create a simple internal form, follow DevOps-style monitoring and feedback to improve it, and use cloud services to host parts of the solution. These choices can complement one another, but each introduces distinct questions about integration, maintainability, security or operations.\n\nTherefore, engineers should identify the actual problem first and select the combination of approaches that best fits its requirements and constraints."}, {"topic": "Synthesis", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 12, "slides": "7, 11, 13–15, 28", "question": "Discuss why modern software engineering is about more than learning new programming languages or tools.", "points": ["Systematic engineering foundation", "Multiple technologies and teams", "Lifecycle and operations", "Evaluation and verification", "Real-world consequences"], "answer": "Modern software engineering is about applying systematic engineering judgement to increasingly complex systems, rather than simply learning the latest programming language. The lecture identifies cloud, mobile platforms, APIs, microservices, DevOps, AI, IoT, edge computing, cybersecurity, big data and distributed teams as parts of the modern environment.\n\nA developer must understand how components work together and how software can be developed, tested, deployed, monitored and maintained. DevOps shows that the engineering lifecycle continues after software is released. AI-assisted development also changes the engineer’s role toward specifying, evaluating, verifying and orchestrating work rather than writing every line manually.\n\nFurthermore, emerging technologies introduce risks alongside benefits. For example, microservices can bring distributed complexity, while AI-generated code may be insecure. Engineers need to choose technologies for suitable problems and manage their limitations.\n\nTherefore, professional capability is demonstrated by understanding requirements, selecting appropriate approaches and delivering dependable solutions. The value lies in how technologies are engineered and applied, not merely in the number of tools a developer knows."}, {"topic": "Synthesis", "kind": "Propose", "marks": 12, "slides": "18, 21–22, 25, 28–29", "question": "Propose a smart-campus software system using two emerging technologies. Discuss benefits, limitations and realistic adoption.", "points": ["Specific campus problem", "Two correct technology applications", "Benefit for each", "New engineering challenges", "Feasibility decision"], "answer": "I propose a smart-campus building-monitoring system that uses IoT sensors and a digital twin. The problem is that campus facilities staff may need better information about changing conditions within different buildings. IoT devices could provide data about selected building conditions, while a digital twin could present an updated digital representation of the physical facilities.\n\nThe main potential benefit is improved visibility into how the buildings are operating. A digital twin can be updated using information from the real system, making it more useful than a static representation. However, the lecture identifies security as a concern for IoT, and it asks how engineers can integrate, test and maintain digital twins that change with the physical environment.\n\nBefore adopting the system, I would clarify which building problem needs solving and whether appropriate sensor data is available. The team would need to evaluate software integration, security and maintenance demands. I would recommend a limited implementation for one building first, followed by testing and evaluation before a wider rollout. This supports practical learning without assuming the technologies automatically provide value."}, {"topic": "Synthesis", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 12, "slides": "12–13, 19–20, 24–25", "question": "“AI will replace software engineers because it can generate code and act as an agent.” Evaluate this statement.", "points": ["Explain assistance and agents", "Role change not elimination", "Verification and orchestration", "Control/trust/security", "Balanced conclusion"], "answer": "The statement overlooks the lecture’s distinction between producing software outputs and performing software engineering responsibly. AI can support code generation, code completion, test creation, documentation and review. AI agents can go further by planning, using tools, performing actions, evaluating results and adapting to goals.\n\nHowever, the lecture explicitly states that AI does not remove the need for software engineering. Instead, the developer’s role shifts toward specifying what is needed, evaluating results, verifying correctness and orchestrating tools and activities. Generated code can be incorrect, insecure or unsuitable, and agentic actions raise additional questions about control and trust.\n\nFor example, an AI may rapidly produce an implementation, but an engineer still needs to decide whether it meets requirements, whether it integrates with other components and whether it behaves safely. The ability to automate work may change task allocation and productivity, but it does not automatically solve those engineering questions.\n\nTherefore, AI is more accurately viewed in this lecture as changing the nature of software engineering work, rather than removing the need for skilled engineers and professional judgement."}, {"topic": "Synthesis", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 12, "slides": "5, 10, 24–25, 30", "question": "Explain the lecture’s take-home message: engineers must know not only WHAT a technology does, but WHEN and HOW to apply it.", "points": ["WHAT = capabilities", "WHEN = suitable problem/context", "HOW = design/integration/testing", "Limitations/new risks", "Concluding principle"], "answer": "Knowing what a technology does is only the first part of software engineering evaluation. For example, a student may know that edge computing processes information near the device or that generative AI can generate code. These definitions describe capabilities but do not establish whether the technology is suitable for a particular project.\n\nKnowing when to use a technology requires understanding the problem and the circumstances in which its benefits matter. Edge computing may be useful when a system needs rapid local decisions, while a low-code platform may be appropriate for a limited application requiring quick visual development. Knowing how to apply the technology involves designing the system, integrating components, testing behaviour and planning ongoing maintenance.\n\nEngineers must also assess limitations and new challenges. Edge computing introduces distributed complexity, generative AI can produce insecure output, and low-code can raise vendor lock-in and maintainability concerns.\n\nThus, the lecture’s main message is that software engineering is evolving, but responsible technology selection still depends on well-reasoned engineering decisions."}], "mcq": [{"question": "Which definition best matches the lecture’s software engineering foundation?", "choices": ["Ad-hoc code writing only", "Systematic engineering approaches to developing, operating and maintaining software", "Using the newest programming language", "Deploying without maintenance"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Slide 5 defines SE as a systematic engineering approach across development, operation and maintenance.", "slides": "5"}, {"question": "Which sequence matches the traditional process in the lecture?", "choices": ["Requirements → Design → Implementation → Testing → Deployment → Maintenance", "Code → Deploy → Ignore feedback", "Monitor → Design → Code", "Cloud → AI → IoT"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "The staged process on slide 6 begins with requirements and ends with maintenance.", "slides": "6"}, {"question": "Which is a driver of change under the SOCIETY category?", "choices": ["Only code completion", "Only price discounts", "Privacy, accessibility, sustainability and trust", "Compiler optimisation"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "Slide 9 lists privacy, sustainability, accessibility and trust under society.", "slides": "9"}, {"question": "What does slide 10 say normally follows a new technological capability?", "choices": ["All risks disappear", "New user expectations", "Guaranteed software quality", "No further engineering"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "The chain is technology → capability → expectation → new software problem.", "slides": "10"}, {"question": "Which is NOT one of the ten trends on slide 11?", "choices": ["Platform engineering", "Software supply-chain security", "Human–AI collaboration", "Replacing all software testing"], "correct": 3, "explanation": "The lecture lists ten trends; it never lists elimination of software testing.", "slides": "11"}, {"question": "What is the emerging role of a developer in AI-assisted engineering?", "choices": ["Only manually typing code", "Specifying, evaluating, verifying and orchestrating", "Avoiding all AI tools", "Delegating accountability to AI"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Slide 13 emphasises specification, evaluation, verification and orchestration.", "slides": "13"}, {"question": "Which DevOps sequence is correct?", "choices": ["Plan → Code → Build → Test → Release → Deploy → Monitor → Feedback", "Code → Deploy → Plan → Ignore", "Design → Buy → Deploy", "Test → Monitor → Stop"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "Slide 14 explicitly lists the eight-stage continuous flow.", "slides": "14"}, {"question": "Which is a cloud-native component in the lecture?", "choices": ["Containers", "Paper-only procedures", "Manual registers only", "No APIs"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "Slide 15 names containers, microservices, APIs, elasticity, distributed systems and continuous deployment.", "slides": "15"}, {"question": "Which is an engineering concern of low-code/no-code?", "choices": ["Guaranteed portability", "Vendor lock-in", "No need for security", "Infinite scalability"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Slide 16 identifies vendor lock-in among five engineering concerns.", "slides": "16"}, {"question": "Which best distinguishes an emerging technology?", "choices": ["It has never been demonstrated", "Its capabilities, applications or adoption are still developing", "It must be free", "It is always safer"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Slide 17 defines emerging technology by still-developing capabilities, applications or adoption.", "slides": "17"}, {"question": "Which sequence best represents an AI agent?", "choices": ["User → AI → Answer only", "Goal → Plan → Use tools → Perform actions → Evaluate → Adapt", "Build → Deploy → Forget", "Data → Static image"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "The agentic workflow on slide 20 includes planning, tool use, actions, evaluation and adaptation.", "slides": "20"}, {"question": "What makes a digital twin more than a static drawing?", "choices": ["It is always 3D", "It is updated using data from the physical system", "It needs no maintenance", "It exists only for factories"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "The slide defines a digital twin using data updates from a real physical system.", "slides": "21"}, {"question": "Which benefit of edge computing is explicitly identified?", "choices": ["Guaranteed privacy", "Lower latency", "Elimination of distributed systems", "Universal quantum acceleration"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Slide 22 lists lower latency, reduced bandwidth, local processing and privacy considerations.", "slides": "22"}, {"question": "What warning does the lecture give about quantum computing?", "choices": ["It is merely a faster ordinary computer", "It will replace every cloud service", "It is not simply a faster computer; new models may change algorithm design", "It is only a mobile app"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "Slide 23 distinguishes new computing models from general-purpose speed increases.", "slides": "23"}, {"question": "Which technology-to-concern pairing matches slide 24?", "choices": ["AI agents → control / trust", "IoT → no security issues", "Edge → no distributed complexity", "GenAI → guaranteed correctness"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "Slide 24 pairs agentic automation with control/trust concerns.", "slides": "24"}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is software engineering?", "back": "Systematic engineering applied to software development, operation and maintenance.", "slides": "5"}, {"front": "What stays constant as software evolves?", "back": "Fundamental software engineering principles.", "slides": "5"}, {"front": "Name the traditional stages.", "back": "Requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, maintenance.", "slides": "6"}, {"front": "Name the modern continuous stages.", "back": "Requirements, development, testing, deployment, monitoring, feedback.", "slides": "6"}, {"front": "What technologies represent “today”?", "back": "Cloud, mobile, AI, IoT and distributed systems.", "slides": "4"}, {"front": "What future directions appear on slide 4?", "back": "AI agents, AI-generated software and intelligent systems.", "slides": "4"}, {"front": "Name the four drivers of change.", "back": "Technology, business, users and society.", "slides": "9"}, {"front": "What is the speed-of-change chain?", "back": "Technology → capability → expectation → new software problem.", "slides": "10"}, {"front": "What is AI-assisted development?", "back": "Using AI to support code, tests, requirements, documentation and review.", "slides": "12"}, {"front": "What is the developer’s emerging role?", "back": "Specify, evaluate, verify and orchestrate.", "slides": "13"}, {"front": "Why must AI-generated code be checked?", "back": "It can be incorrect, insecure or inappropriate.", "slides": "27"}, {"front": "What does DevOps integrate?", "back": "Development and operations across the software lifecycle.", "slides": "14"}, {"front": "List the eight DevOps steps.", "back": "Plan, Code, Build, Test, Release, Deploy, Monitor, Feedback.", "slides": "14"}, {"front": "Name the six cloud-native elements.", "back": "Containers, microservices, APIs, elasticity, distributed systems, continuous deployment.", "slides": "15"}, {"front": "What is a microservices architecture?", "back": "An application organised into smaller services with distinct responsibilities.", "slides": "15"}, {"front": "Which five example services appear?", "back": "User, Payment, Recommendation, Notification and Order.", "slides": "15"}, {"front": "What is cloud’s benefit–concern pair?", "back": "Scalability versus complexity.", "slides": "24"}, {"front": "What does low-code/no-code use?", "back": "Visual development and drag-and-drop components.", "slides": "16"}, {"front": "What is a citizen developer?", "back": "A person creating applications without necessarily being a professional programmer.", "slides": "16"}, {"front": "Name five low-code concerns.", "back": "Security, scalability, maintainability, vendor lock-in, integration.", "slides": "16"}, {"front": "What defines an emerging technology?", "back": "Its capabilities, applications or adoption are still developing.", "slides": "17"}, {"front": "Name the four technology landscape areas.", "back": "AI, Computing, Connected Systems and Development.", "slides": "18"}, {"front": "Name three emerging AI examples.", "back": "Generative AI, AI agents and multimodal AI.", "slides": "18"}, {"front": "Which six stages can GenAI support?", "back": "Requirements, design, coding, testing, documentation, maintenance.", "slides": "19"}, {"front": "How does traditional AI interact?", "back": "User → AI → Answer.", "slides": "20"}, {"front": "How does an AI agent operate?", "back": "Goal → Plan → Tools → Actions → Evaluate → Adapt.", "slides": "20"}, {"front": "What concern accompanies AI-agent automation?", "back": "Control and trust.", "slides": "24"}, {"front": "What is a digital twin?", "back": "A digital model of a physical system updated using real-system data.", "slides": "21"}, {"front": "Name three digital-twin examples.", "back": "Factory, building, vehicle, city or healthcare equipment.", "slides": "21"}, {"front": "What is the main digital twin challenge?", "back": "Developing, integrating, testing and maintaining continuously updated software.", "slides": "21"}, {"front": "What is the cloud data path?", "back": "Device → Internet → Cloud.", "slides": "22"}, {"front": "What is the edge data path?", "back": "Device → Nearby computing → Cloud.", "slides": "22"}, {"front": "Why use edge computing?", "back": "Lower latency, less bandwidth and local processing.", "slides": "22"}, {"front": "What concern accompanies edge computing?", "back": "Distributed complexity.", "slides": "24"}, {"front": "What are four potential quantum areas?", "back": "Optimisation, cryptography, simulation and machine learning.", "slides": "23"}, {"front": "What is quantum computing NOT simply?", "back": "A faster conventional computer.", "slides": "23"}, {"front": "What is GenAI’s benefit–concern pair?", "back": "Faster development versus hallucination/security.", "slides": "24"}, {"front": "What is IoT’s benefit–concern pair?", "back": "Connected systems versus security risk.", "slides": "24"}, {"front": "What is low-code’s benefit–concern pair?", "back": "Rapid development versus maintainability concerns.", "slides": "24"}, {"front": "What do the six evaluation questions begin with?", "back": "What problem? How does it work? When useful?", "slides": "25"}, {"front": "What do the final evaluation questions cover?", "back": "Limitations, new engineering problems and realistic adoption.", "slides": "25"}, {"front": "What does the course ask engineers to do?", "back": "Explore, understand, analyse, evaluate, apply and synthesise.", "slides": "29"}, {"front": "What is the lecture’s three-part takeaway?", "back": "SE evolves; new technology brings challenges; know what, when and how to apply.", "slides": "30"}]};
